{"book":{"id":"book-giving-voice-mobile-communication-disability-and-inequality-meryl-alper","slug":"giving-voice-mobile-communication-disability-and-inequality-meryl-alper","title":"Giving Voice: Mobile Communication, Disability, and Inequality","authors":[{"id":"person-meryl-alper","name":"Meryl Alper"}],"publisherId":"publisher-mit-press","imprintId":"imprint-mit-press","isbn13":["9780262533973"],"subjects":["Technology"],"topics":["Technology, Computing & AI","Class, Poverty & Inequality","Disability & Difference","Family, Childhood & Adoption"],"centralFigures":[],"links":{"amazon":"https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Giving%20Voice%3A%20Mobile%20Communication%2C%20Disability%2C%20and%20Inequality%20Meryl%20Alper","bookshop":"https://bookshop.org/search?keywords=Giving%20Voice%3A%20Mobile%20Communication%2C%20Disability%2C%20and%20Inequality%20Meryl%20Alper","indiebound":"https://www.indiebound.org/search/book?keys=Giving%20Voice%3A%20Mobile%20Communication%2C%20Disability%2C%20and%20Inequality%20Meryl%20Alper","worldcat":"https://search.worldcat.org/search?q=Giving%20Voice%3A%20Mobile%20Communication%2C%20Disability%2C%20and%20Inequality%20Meryl%20Alper","publisher":"https://openlibrary.org/works/OL20058238W"},"sourceIds":["source-award-record-prose-media-and-cultural-studies-prose-awards-2018-winners-archive","source-imprint-normalization","source-open-library-giving-voice-mobile-communication-disability-and-inequality-meryl-alper","source-google-books-giving-voice-mobile-communication-disability-and-inequality-meryl-alper","source-open-library-lcc-giving-voice-mobile-communication-disability-and-inequality-meryl-alper"],"publicationYear":2017,"pageCount":288,"summary":"How communication technologies meant to empower people with speech disorders—to give voice to the voiceless—are still subject to disempowering structural inequalities. Mobile technologies are often hailed as a way to “give voice to the voiceless.” Behind the praise, though, are beliefs about technology as a gateway to opportunity and voice as a metaphor for agency and self-representation. In Giving Voice, Meryl Alper explores these assumptions by looking closely at one such case—the use of the Apple iPad and mobile app Proloquo2Go, which converts icons and text into synthetic speech, by children with disabilities (including autism and cerebral palsy) and their families. She finds that despite claims to empowerment, the hardware and software are still subject to disempowering structural inequalities.","thumbnailUrl":"/book-covers/book-giving-voice-mobile-communication-disability-and-inequality-meryl-alper.jpg","libraryShelf":{"scheme":"lcc","callNumber":"HV1568.4 .A47 2017","rawCallNumber":"HV1568.4 .A47 2017","mainClass":"H","subclass":"HV","completeness":"full_call_number","confidence":"high","matchedBy":"open_library_exact_isbn","sourceId":"source-open-library-lcc-giving-voice-mobile-communication-disability-and-inequality-meryl-alper","sourceEditionId":"/books/OL27238246M","sourceWorkId":"/works/OL20058238W","sourceIsbn13":"9780262533973","sort":{"classLetters":"HV","classWholeNumber":1568,"classDecimalDigits":"4","cutters":[{"letters":"A","decimalDigits":"47"}],"year":2017}},"readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"crossover","narrativeScore":0,"accessibilityScore":0,"scholarlyScore":0,"traits":[{"id":"essayistic","score":1,"confidence":"high","evidence":["catalog text contains \"essay\"","catalog text contains \"essays\"","catalog text contains \"criticism\"","literature/criticism subject or topic"]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Despite claims that mobile speech technologies empower people with speech disorders by “giving voice,” the iPad and Proloquo2Go use embedded beliefs that reproduce structural inequalities and can disempower users.","confidence":0.72},"academicOrientation":{"score":70,"confidence":0.62},"profileConfidence":0.84,"mod